// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// mm_comp.js · THE COMP, the show's compositing kit (first built for EP11; every episode from EP11 on loads it) (🗣 Mook, 2026-10-14 5:37 pm: "seeing the water at the lake im realizing we dont have
//   compositing in our pipeline! Comping is like the most important part of vfx that should help just putting a focus on that
//   for boarding like how to comp it using vfx tricks"). 🎬 Claude.
//   ONE IDEA: a shot is a STACK OF LAYERS, not one painting. Every shot renders its PLATE (the set: what's there without the
//   cast) and its BEAUTY (the same frame with the cast); the CAST LAYER is cut out of the beauty where the two differ, so it
//   carries every occlusion the painter already got right (a reed in front of a leg stays in front). Then the tricks a
//   compositor uses to sit a character into a plate are each one canvas operation on a layer:
//    · GRADE        the plate and the cast each get their own colour (lift, saturation, warmth) so they sit together
//    · DEFOCUS      the plate a little soft behind the cast (or the cast soft in front): depth, and a stiff pose reads less
//    · LIGHT WRAP   the plate's light bleeds in round the cast's edges (the most important integration trick)
//    · CONTACT      a soft dark under every foot (contact shadow) and the cast's own shape laid long on the ground away from
//                   the suns (cast shadow): nobody floats
//    · RIM          a thin bright edge on the side toward the light
//    · SMEAR        a fast move smeared along its direction (a vector motion blur): a move reads, the stiff frames between
//                   don't
//    · HOLDOUT      something in FRONT of the cast cut back over it (a door's frame, a floor round a hatch): layering by
//                   matte, never by sorting luck
//    · TRANSITIONS  two shots in one frame, mixed through a matte (a shape, a luma key, a bloom): the switch IS a comp
//   Everything is canvas operations (drawImage, composite modes, scaled-down blurs): GeoV's loop guard never sees a loop
//   but one: the plate/beauty difference, done once a frame inside a block function.
//   HOW AN EPISODE USES IT: load it after its sets (e9.use('…/ep03/blocks/mm_comp.js')); in a shot, call
//   mmcComp(g, o)  o = { scene(gg, withCast): draws the frame into gg and RETURNS ITS LENS C (the plate when withCast is
//     false), grade: { plate, cast } (lift / sat / warm), defocus: k (1/k blur), ramp: [y full blur, y sharp], defocusK,
//     wrap: 0…1, wrapPx, rim: { dir: [x, y], k, col }, feet: [[x, y, z, w m, k]] (in the world, through C → contact + the
//     shadow's base line), contact: [{ x, y, w, k, squash }] (on the screen), shadow: { dx, sy (<0 = toward the lens), k,
//     col, base }, smear: [dx, dy] px, smearN, thr, holdout(gg): draws what stands in front, after(gg) }
//     → returns { plate, cast, beauty }
//   mmcMix(g, A, B, matte, { soft, edge })  two shots through a matte: A(gg), B(gg) draw the shots, matte(gg) draws white
//     where B shows · mmcLuma(src, dark, k) a luma key (the darks first) · mmcBloom(g, src, k, px) the bright parts, glowing
//   BOARDING: every set-up's sheet line gets a COMP line under it (its layers, its tricks, its holdout matte); the switches
//   and the doors are boarded as comps of their own. Needs ydPt (the lens, mm_yard3.js). Cost: two renders + ~1 s of
